<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A marketplace in Mexico sold electronics, home goods and fashion from thousands of sellers, with deliveries handled by several carriers. Its contact centre took calls in Spanish, and most of them were about the same few things: where an order was, when it would arrive, how to change the address, how to start a return. During sales seasons, queues grew long enough that callers hung up and tried again, which made the queues longer.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> On 29 October 2026, Deepgram announced it is acquiring Daily, the company behind Pipecat, a widely used open-source framework for real-time voice agents<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Both companies said Pipecat will stay under its BSD licence and move to an independent foundation whose steering committee includes Deepgram's competitors<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. For a team choosing a voice agent stack, that settled the main worry about building on an open framework. On the legal side, Mexico's new personal data protection law took effect in March 2025<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, and legal commentary notes a right to object to automated processing that significantly affects people without human involvement<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Callers would always need a route to a person.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Natural Mexican Spanish</strong>, including regional accents, background noise and code-switching with English product names.</li>
<li><strong>Conversational speed</strong>: replies quick enough that silence never feels like a dropped call.</li>
<li><strong>Grounded answers only</strong>: order status from the systems, never from the model's imagination.</li>
<li><strong>A person on request</strong>, with the conversation so far passed across.</li>
<li><strong>Clear data rules</strong> for recordings and transcripts, and no lock-in to one speech vendor.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Caller</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Phone<em>SIP trunk</em></span><span>App call button<em>WebRTC</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Voice pipeline</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Pipecat<em>streaming orchestration</em></span><span>Speech-to-text<em>streaming, Spanish models</em></span><span>Text-to-speech<em>streaming, Mexican voice</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Reasoning</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">LLM with tool calls<em>structured, narrow tools</em></span><span>Turn-taking<em>interruptions, silence</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Tools</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Order lookup<em>verified caller only</em></span><span>Carrier tracking<em>live status</em></span><span>Address change and returns<em>rules first</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>People</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Handover to agents<em>summary and transcript</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Assure</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Per-stage tracing<em>OpenTelemetry</em></span><span>Evaluations<em>real call samples</em></span><span>Retention rules<em>recordings and transcripts</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The framework streams every stage; tools ground every answer; people are one sentence away.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Answer</b><span>The call connects; the agent says it is an automated assistant and offers a person</span></li>
<li><b>Verify</b><span>The caller is matched to an order with phone number and a light check</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Understand</b><span>Streaming speech recognition feeds the model as the caller speaks</span></li>
<li><b>Look up</b><span>The model calls order and carrier tools; it never guesses a status</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Reply</b><span>Speech starts streaming as soon as the first words are ready</span></li>
<li><b>Hand over</b><span>On request or low confidence, a person takes over with the summary</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Streaming at every stage is what makes the pause short enough to feel human.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: latency you can feel</h2>
<p>In text chat, a two-second delay is fine. On a phone call it feels like the line has dropped. The delay a caller hears is the sum of several stages: detecting that they stopped speaking, finishing speech recognition, the model's first tokens, any tool calls, and the first audio from speech synthesis. Each stage looked acceptable alone; together they were too slow.</p>
<p>The team set a latency budget per stage and traced every call end to end, so each turn showed exactly where the time went, in the same way distributed tracing shows which service in a request is slow<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. That made the fixes obvious: stream everything instead of waiting for whole sentences; prefetch the caller's recent orders as soon as they are verified, so the first tool call is usually instant; use a smaller, faster model for routine turns; and play a short natural acknowledgement while a slow carrier API responds.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/mexico-ecommerce-voice-ai-agents/trace-latency.webp" alt="Trace view showing a request's entity map across frontend, cart, product catalogue, currency and feature flag services, with a span timeline below listing each call and its duration" width="1400" height="760" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A trace shows where every millisecond of a request goes; the same view, applied to each conversational turn, made latency fixable. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/demo-skyscanner/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Jordi Bisbal Ansaldo</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Turn-taking was the other half. Callers interrupt, pause mid-sentence and talk over the agent. The framework's interruption handling let the agent stop speaking when the caller started, and tuning silence detection for Spanish speech patterns cut the most awkward moments. See <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">model routing</a> and <a href="/insights/prompt-caching-llm-cost-latency/">prompt caching for latency</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Voice agents are judged in hundreds of milliseconds. Budget latency per stage, trace every turn, and stream everything that can be streamed.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Customer operations</small><ol><li>Top call reasons</li><li class="hl">Handover rules</li><li>Agent coaching</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering</small><ol><li>Voice pipeline</li><li class="hl">Latency budget and tracing</li><li>Order and carrier tools</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Legal and privacy</small><ol><li>Disclosure script</li><li>Recording retention</li><li class="hl">Objection and access requests</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Operations defined what the agent should handle; engineering made it fast; legal made it fair.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Listen</b><ul><li>Call reason analysis</li><li>Evaluation set from real calls</li><li>Provider tests in Spanish</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Build</b><ul><li>Pipecat pipeline</li><li>Order and carrier tools</li><li>Handover</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Tune</b><ul><li>Latency budget</li><li>Turn-taking</li><li>Shadow calls</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Expand</b><ul><li>Off-peak first</li><li>Sales-season readiness</li><li>Returns flow</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Off-peak hours first, then sales seasons once the latency budget held.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Open-source framework</td><td>A closed voice agent platform</td><td>Swap speech and model providers; no lock-in</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stream every stage</td><td>Sentence-by-sentence processing</td><td>The only way to stay inside the latency budget</td></tr>
<tr><td>Narrow tools for order data</td><td>Give the model raw order records</td><td>Grounded answers and less personal data exposed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disclose AI and offer a person upfront</td><td>Only hand over on failure</td><td>Trust, and respects the right to object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Off-peak rollout first</td><td>Launch for a sales season</td><td>Learn under low risk before peak load</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: build the evaluation set from real call recordings before choosing providers. Speech models that tested well on clean audio struggled with real calls. Differently: trace latency per stage from the first prototype, not after the first complaints. Related: <a href="/insights/evaluating-ai-agents-reliability/">evaluating AI agents</a>, <a href="/case-studies/agent-ready-travel-marketplace-sea/">an agent-ready travel marketplace</a>, today's <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-31/">tech news</a> and <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
<p>If you are building voice AI for customers in Mexico or elsewhere in Latin America, see my <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
